==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
Confer {{etyl|fr}} ''[[désagréable]]''. {{etystub}} As if {{prefix|dis|agreeable}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|[dɪsəˈgɹiəbəɫ]}}
* {{audio|En-us-disagreeable.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Not [[agreeable]], [[conformable]], or [[congruous]]; [[contrary]]; [[unsuitable]].
#: {{rfdate}} ''Preach you truly the doctrine which you have received, and teach nothing that is '''disagreeable''' thereunto.'' --Udall.
# Exciting [[repugnance]]; [[offensive]] to the feelings or senses; [[displeasing]]; [[unpleasant]].
#: {{rfdate}} ''That which is '''disagreeable''' to one is many times agreeable to another, or '''disagreeable''' in a less degree.'' --Wollaston.

====Usage notes====
* Nouns to which "disagreeable" is often applied: odor, smell, taste, sensation, thing, person, man, duty, work, feeling, manner, experience, effect, feature, business, surprise, woman, job.

====Antonyms====
* [[agreeable]]

